From: edde746 <86283021+edde746@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 08:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] vo_avfoundation: rasterize the inline OSD layer on the GPU

Heavy ASS typesetting played noticeably worse on Apple TV than on
players that composite subtitles on the GPU. The inline OSD layer's
buffer is display-sized (3840x2160 on a 4K output), and every changed
subtitle frame was cleared and blended into it on the VO thread, one
part at a time on the CPU, before the video sample could be enqueued.
Animated signs change every frame, so that cost landed on every frame.

Draw the part list with Metal instead. Copy the parts' bitmaps into a
shelf-packed atlas texture (a linear buffer, so the upload is row
copies; mpv's own atlas can be wider than any Apple GPU texture) and
draw each part as a textured quad into the pool buffer (IOSurface-backed
and Metal-compatible already), with premultiplied source-over blending:
the composition blend_libass_bgra() and blend_bgra_over_bgra() perform.
The atlas is capped at about 32 MiB; a frame with more coverage is drawn
in further passes, in part order. The first pass clears the target, so
recycled buffers need no CPU clear. The draw waits for the GPU before
returning, keeping the OSD-then-video enqueue order that presents
subtitle changes on their frame.

Only the pixels of the OSD layer's buffers change. The video samples,
and with them Dolby Vision and HDR passthrough, are untouched, and the
OSD stays a separate, timebase-driven AVSampleBufferDisplayLayer. The
composite paths (composite-osd, PiP) keep the CPU buffer they read back.
The CPU path remains the fallback when Metal setup or a render fails,
and --avfoundation-osd-gpu=no selects it outright. osd-perf now reports
the Metal renders with their upload and GPU time.

Measured on an Apple TV 4K (A15), Dolby Vision 4K episode, 4K OSD. A
synthetic track of about 200 full-width blurred parts per frame renders
in 52 ms instead of 59-61 ms (interleaved runs), 12 of it the atlas copy
and 38 GPU execution, so it stays over a 24 fps frame. Ordinary dialogue
and signs cost 4-9 ms per render on either path.

